WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.220
Kong: Skull Island is a thrilling reimagining
of King Kong's origins within Warner Bros

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.990
and Legendary's MonsterVerse.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.760
And while Skull Island is packed with awesome
action scenes and monsters, there are still

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.740
a number of fascinating creatures and scenes
including alternate endings and post-credits

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.269
which didn't make it into the final movie.

00:00:17.269 --> 00:00:20.980
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video, I'll be talking about many

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.839
of the scenes and rejected concepts we didn't
get to see in the final cut of Kong: Skull

00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:25.839
Island.

00:00:25.839 --> 00:00:30.039
If you haven't seen the movie yet, obviously
there are spoilers in this video, so be aware

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:34.460
of that or check out my spoiler-free review
of the movie by tapping or clicking here.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.219
Kong: Skull Island has a fantastic opening
scene, however, what director Jordan Vogt-Roberts

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:42.660
initially had planned could have been even
more spectacular.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.130
In an idea that the director put to the studio,
a squad of soldiers arrive on Skull Island

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:50.059
in the middle of World War II, and start fighting
each other.

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:55.270
Suddenly, a huge ape appears that heavily
resembles King Kong from the 2005 Peter Jackson

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.270
movie.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.609
The soldiers shoot the giant gorilla dead,
leaving the audience thinking that they just

00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:01.070
killed Kong himself.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:04.140
But then an even larger ape emerges with a
roar!

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.220
By killing off Jackson's version of Kong in
this alternate opening, Vogt-Roberts wanted

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.270
to establish the idea that the new movie was
going to be very different to previous King

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.270
Kong movies.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:17.020
However, unsurprisingly, the studio was not
as keen as the director on kicking the film

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.750
off in that way!

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.440
Skull Island was also going to feature a number
of other creatures and monsters.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.170
The first creatures that were cut from the
final movie were a pair of sabretooth tigers

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:30.460
that would have appeared the day after Conrad's
team and the Sky Devils military unit landed

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:31.570
on the island.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:35.540
Two enormous sabretooth tigers were going
to enter the camp they'd established, triggering

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.510
a soldier to shoot and kill one of them, which
would have sent the second tiger berserk,

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:40.970
killing that soldier.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.950
The scene was an alternative way of introducing
the idea that the explorers were setting in

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.700
motion events that would disrupt a local ecosystem
they had no idea about.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.890
Other weird and wonderful creatures cut from
the movie include a monster Venus flytrap.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:58.230
By the way, interestingly, a Venus flytrap
monster already appeared in the animated Kong

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:02.230
TV series where it battled and nearly defeated
Kong in the episode "Green Fear".

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:07.860
And The Art And Making of Kong: Skull Island
has some very cool unused concept art of other

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.920
creatures and monsters we never got to see
in the final film.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.390
A number of unused designs exist for creatures
below the water and others that live on the

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.940
land.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:22.640
Although dinosaurs have featured in previous
Kong films such as the 1933 and 2005 movies,

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.400
Vogt-Roberts was adamant that dinosaurs shouldn't
appear in Kong: Skull Island.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.020
The director said he didn't want to try and
rival the Jurassic Park and World movies,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.310
and he wanted his creatures not to feel "too
alien-like."

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:38.420
In the end, the closest we got to a dinosaur
in the final movie was a triceratops skull.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.470
Director Vogt-Roberts also wanted the indigenous
population and where they lived to feel fresh

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.610
and different from previous Kong movies.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.880
But before the Iwi village we see in the final
movie, two alternative indigenous settlements

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:49.880
were planned.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:53.640
The first was going to be the Boat City where
people lived among boats and other ships that

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.340
had washed up on the island.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.220
The second village was going to be known as
Kong City and it was planned as a traditional

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.900
tribal village that was built into rocks and
caverns.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.130
It was a place where the people literally
worshipped Kong who would sit like a king

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:07.730
on a throne in front of them.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:11.720
The concept art for Kong City looks epic and
although it didn't make it into the final

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.920
movie, there is a nod to the original idea
in the stone paintings of the Iwi worshipping

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.480
Kong that we see after Conrad and the team
meet Marlow.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.260
A different ending to the movie was also considered
which would have featured a bigger role for

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:24.650
the Iwi people.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:28.540
The alternate ending involved a desperate
action scene with the team stuck on Marlow's

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.400
boat while being attacked by a Skull Crawler.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.709
In the scene, the Skull Crawlers have broken
through the wall and the boat ends up trapped

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:37.350
at the dead end of the river.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:42.360
The only way to escape is for the Iwi villagers
to haul the boat up over a rock face and waterfall

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.430
and into another river to get them to safety.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.349
The scene was inspired by director Werner
Herzog's 1982 film, Fitzcarraldo, about a

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:54.790
rubber baron who plans to use native people
to drag a massive steamship over the side

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:57.380
of a mountain in the Amazon Basin.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.240
As well as an alternate ending, there were
also alternate post-credits scenes.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:06.020
In one of them, Conrad and Weaver end up on
a boat in the Arctic Ocean together with Brooks.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:10.380
As they watch the ocean, Godzilla suddenly
rises up through the layer of ice.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:13.660
Although this would have been pretty cool,
the idea was rejected as they realised the

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:18.659
scene would have been inconsistent with the
timeline set in the 2014 Godzilla movie.

00:04:18.659 --> 00:04:23.430
The main story in Kong: Skull Island takes
place in the 1970s and in the Godzilla film,

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:27.189
it was reported that Godzilla hadn't appeared
since the atomic bomb tests of the 1950s.

00:04:27.189 --> 00:04:31.560
Now, I'd love to know what you thought about
the deleted scenes and creatures we never

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.620
got to see in Kong: Skull Island.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.659
And are there any kaiju you'd love to see
appear in a future Kong or Godzilla movie?

00:04:37.659 --> 00:04:39.550
